# Aarogya Architecture Rules

## Architectural Principle

Separate the application into clear layers.

Presentation
↓
Application / State
↓
Domain
↓
Data
↓
Sensors / Communication / Storage / Edge AI

Business logic must not live inside UI widgets.

## Recommended Modules

The application should conceptually contain:

• onboarding
• localization
• health
• baseline
• sensors
• edge_ai
• disaster
• survival
• emergency
• communication
• location
• maps
• settings

The exact folder structure may change if there is a strong architectural reason.

## Domain Layer

Domain logic should not depend directly on:

• Flutter widgets
• Android UI
• Network clients
• Specific sensor SDKs
• Specific database implementations

Use interfaces where appropriate.

Examples:

HealthRepository

SensorSource

InferenceEngine

LocationService

ConnectivityService

EmergencyRepository

CommunicationTransport

## Sensor Abstraction

Real sensors and simulated sensors must use the same interface.

Example:

SensorSource
├── WearableSensorSource
├── DeviceSensorSource
└── SimulatedSensorSource

The domain layer should not care which implementation provides the data.

## Edge AI Abstraction

Inference should be behind an interface.

Example:

InferenceEngine

Implementations can include:

LocalInferenceEngine

MockInferenceEngine

The UI must not directly invoke model files.

## Disaster State Machine

Emergency states must be explicit.

Suggested model:

NORMAL
→ DISASTER_ALERT
→ DISASTER_ACTIVE
→ SURVIVAL
→ EMERGENCY_EVENT
→ RESOLVED

The exact implementation may differ.

State transitions must be centralized.

Do not scatter state flags such as:

isFlood

isEmergency

isSurvival

isFall

across unrelated widgets.

## State Integrity

An active emergency must survive:

• Screen changes
• App backgrounding
• Temporary connectivity loss
• Language changes
• Sensor reconnection

Where required, emergency state must be persisted locally.

## Offline First

Critical paths must have local implementations.

Network availability should be treated as an enhancement, not a prerequisite.

Example:

BAD:

Risk calculation
→ API
→ Risk result

GOOD:

Sensor data
→ local preprocessing
→ local inference
→ local risk result

## Persistence

Use local structured storage for:

• User baseline
• Health history
• Emergency events
• Disaster state
• Pending emergency packets
• User preferences
• Localization preference

Sensitive information must not be stored unnecessarily.

## Networking

Network services should be isolated.

Do not call HTTP APIs directly from UI widgets.

Use repository/service abstractions.

Every network operation must have:

• timeout
• failure handling
• offline handling
• retry strategy where appropriate

## Communication

Create an abstraction for emergency communication.

Example:

CommunicationTransport

Possible implementations:

• InternetTransport
• BLETransport
• WiFiDirectTransport
• SimulatedTransport

This allows the SIH prototype to demonstrate local communication without locking the entire architecture to one technology.

## Dependency Injection

Use predictable dependency injection.

Do not create global mutable singletons unless there is a clear reason.

## Error Handling

Failures must be explicit.

Do not silently swallow exceptions.

Do not silently replace missing sensor data with zero.

Represent missing data explicitly.

## Build Quality

After significant architectural changes:

1. Format code.
2. Run static analysis.
3. Run relevant tests.
4. Build the application.
5. Fix errors before moving to the next feature.
